  "configuration_id": "config_004",
  "reason": "With no prior evidence, the key uncertainty is whether a multimodal, multiprofile pipeline outperforms simpler alternatives, and which component choices (encoder, aggregation, fusion) matter most. Config_004 (multimodal, multiprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, concat) tests the core hypothesis that combining RNA and image modalities with multiple profiles yields strong accuracy, while using relatively simple, well-understood components (PCA for RNA, ResNet50 for image, average-pool aggregation, concatenation fusion). This establishes a solid performance baseline at moderate computational cost—roughly 1–1.5× a single-modality run, and significantly cheaper than ViT-L-based configs. If it performs well, subsequent experiments can systematically upgrade one component at a time (e.g., swap to ViT-L, scvi/scgpt encoders, or transformer aggregation) to identify the highest-accuracy configuration within the 10-experiment budget. If it underperforms, the result still clarifies whether multimodality or multiprofile aggregation is the bottleneck, directing exploration efficiently."
}

